Z||000012||Eric||11-10-2001||12:15 AM||||Those wheels are Team 3 halibrand fake knock-offs (you can see the lugs behind the spinner).  Few bucks for those things, but they're cool cause they have the deep dish look.  On most replicas you'll see that the spinner protrudes from the outside of the rim, so we used shortened A arms to get the look right.  They're 17" just like the one's on the GT 500 in Gone in Sixty Seconds.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MVC-293S.JPG"> <BR>On the inside, we used Classic instruments with the reverse original style speedo (you can see it just to the right of the steering wheel).  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/MVC-281S_1.JPG"> <BR>The engine was from a early '90s mustang 5.0.  When we tore it apart, we didn't like what we saw, so it was off to the machine shop for some "freshening".  The block and crank are the only things that remain from the 5.0.  We threw on some ported World Product iron heads and a nasty custom ground hydro. roller just to add to the craziness.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Resize_Wizard-1_engine_pic.jpg"> <BR>cam sheet:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Resize_Wizard-1_cam_sheet.jpg"> <BR>The car weighs about 2100 lbs without driver, and we're guessin' (haven't dynoed it yet) the motor is making about 330 to 350 horsepower.  My best 1/4 mile was 13.0 @ 106.  That was my first time ever driving the car on the dragstrip.  My second, and last, run was much worse because I nearly crashed spinning in second gear.  The next day Dad was busting out some 1/8 mile times of 8.1 @ 88 at the local track.  He improved on our 1/8 times a lot from the night before. So I imagine mid to high twelves are possible in this car.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||artherd||11-10-2001||01:34 AM||||SWEET!<P>A guy up the street from me has a Shelby Factory replica that he just sold his 'vette to buy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Has a super nice set of 18" wheels, which like yours, I think look **GREAT** on these cars!<P>PS: His car is scary. It's a real 427 (impossible to find anymore) and good for "over 600bhp"  I belive it, it will smoke the (265/40/18) tyres in any gear.<P>One of these days I am going to do a GT40  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>PS: looks great in silver!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000014||rogergarrison||11-10-2001||09:20 AM||||this GT40 kit is much more realistic than the Fiero one, what ya think.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GT40_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Sage||11-10-2001||09:34 AM||||That's super cool in many ways.  A family project is always a good thing, and one that produces a car like that, well there are just no words that adequatly describe how great it is!<P>If you ever cruise down toward Marietta, give me a jingle, (email or p/m me for #) would love to see that in person!  I'll buy you lunch, for a 6 mi. ride on the interstate!LOL<P>Great job!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!||||reg||14
Z||000016||Eric||11-10-2001||10:23 AM||||You're on, Sage.  I can't wait for cruise-in season next year. It's always so much more rewarding talking to real car guys than local bumpkins that catch at a stop light or in a parking lot.  Just before the car was in paint, my dad and I stopped at the local refueling/ rebeering station on the way to a friend's house.  We got a couple stares and a "Nice car, Dude", but there was this one cornball who kept insisting that we were cruising around in the car James Dean was killed in.  My dad was like, "That was a Porsche, this is a Ford."<P>Roger, who makes the kit you're thinking of building?  I know they're only a handful of companies around the world who make GT40s, and I thought they only make racing versions.  Personally, I like the racing versions a little more; they don't seem quite as dated.  The Fiero based GT40s never seem to look quite right.  I think the overall shape and design loses a lot of its character when stretched over a Fiero's chassis.<P>This was taken at Run and Gun '00<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Resize_Wizard-1_GT_40.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||WampusCat||11-10-2001||11:55 AM||||Nice looking ride Eric!  I like that color combo.  One of my navy buddies had a kit Cobra back in the mid 80's and whenever we went to a resturant etc, people would always come outside to ask about the car.  I would love to build either one of those or one of these: <A HREF="http://www.rev-mag.com/Beck550/beck550.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rev-mag.com/Beck550/beck550.htm</A>  or  <A HREF="http://www.beckcars.com/spyder.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.beckcars.com/spyder.shtml</A> <BR>Those Spyders get some pretty good performance from a VW engine.<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000018||Sage||11-10-2001||01:06 PM||||Good deal Eric!  Just let me know when.<P>You like Ryans?  Or we have  Shoneys and many pit stops on "Burger boulevard"<P>Just went to a new place last night for my wife's birthday in Parkersburg,(about a 20 min. ride), called River City.  GREAT food, nice atmosphere, good service.<P>We can figure that out later, just let me know whenever you can make it down this way.<P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!||||reg||14
